## Centralize Login's sign-in ordering in a SignInManager override, and de-duplicate confirmation-email sending

### Status
Fixed.

### GitHub Issue
None yet.

### Summary
#94's username-enumeration fix in `Login.cshtml.cs` hand-rolls a partial reimplementation of
`SignInManager<TUser>.CheckPasswordSignInAsync`/`PreSignInCheck`'s internal ordering directly in
the page handler, instead of through the subclassing extension point Identity provides for
exactly this. That reimplementation is incomplete (lockout is still disclosed before the
password is verified, unlike confirmation status), hashes the password twice per successful
login, and depends on undocumented internal Identity ordering with no compiler-enforced link to
the framework code it mirrors. Separately, the confirmation-email-sending sequence (generate
token → base64url-encode → build callback URL → send email) is now duplicated across
`Register.cshtml.cs`, `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs`, and `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`.

### Motivation
Moving the sign-in reordering into a `SignInManager` subclass override is the idiomatic way
ASP.NET Core Identity expects this kind of customization to be made — it centralizes the
ordering in the one virtual method designed for it, applies uniformly to every caller (not just
this one page), and lets `Login.cshtml.cs` shrink back down to close to its original scaffolded
shape. It also closes the current fix's gaps as a side effect: gating lockout disclosure behind
a correct password (matching how confirmation status is already gated) and hashing the password
only once instead of twice.

Extracting the confirmation-email-sending sequence into one shared service removes three
independent copies that would otherwise have to be kept in sync by hand.

### Proposed Changes
1. Add `ApplicationSignInManager : SignInManager<ApplicationUser>` (proposed location:
   `Models/ApplicationSignInManager.cs`, alongside `IdentityConfiguration.cs`, since this is
   Identity composition rather than a general-purpose `Services/` utility — confirm this
   placement during implementation if a better fit turns up) overriding
   `CheckPasswordSignInAsync`:
   - If `CheckPasswordAsync` fails, return `SignInResult.Failed` immediately — no lockout or
     confirmation state is consulted or disclosed for a wrong password.
   - If it succeeds, run `PreSignInCheck` (confirmation, then lockout, matching the framework's
     own ordering) and return whatever it produces; otherwise reset the lockout counter and
     return `SignInResult.Success`.
   - Confirm the exact base-class method/member names and accessibility (e.g. `PreSignInCheck`,
     `ResetLockout`) against the installed Identity package version before implementing, since
     these are framework internals not guaranteed stable across versions.
2. Register it via the Identity builder's sign-in-manager registration in `Program.cs` (e.g.
   `.AddSignInManager<ApplicationSignInManager>()` — confirm the exact extension method name
   against the installed package version) rather than a manual `AddScoped` mapping, so it
   participates in Identity's builder chain the same way the rest of `Program.cs`'s Identity
   setup does.
3. Simplify `Login.cshtml.cs`'s `OnPostAsync` back down to a single
   `_signInManager.PasswordSignInAsync(Input.UserName, Input.Password, Input.RememberMe, lockoutOnFailure: false)`
   call (the framework resolves the username internally), removing the manual
   `FindByNameAsync`/`IsLockedOutAsync`/`CheckPasswordAsync` pre-checks entirely, and switching on
   the returned `SignInResult` the same way it does today.
4. Add `Services/EmailConfirmationLinkSender.cs` — a small, single-purpose scoped service
   (matching the existing `UniqueCodeGenerator`/`EventDateCleanupService` pattern) that generates
   the confirmation token, encodes it, builds the `ConfirmEmail` callback URL, and sends the
   email. Register it in `Program.cs`.
5. Update `Register.cshtml.cs`, `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs`, and `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`
   to call this service instead of each carrying their own copy of the sequence.

### Acceptance Criteria
- A wrong password never reveals lockout or confirmation status — the response is always the
  same generic "Invalid login attempt," regardless of the account's state.
- A correct password against a locked-out account still redirects to `./Lockout`.
- A correct password against an unconfirmed account still redirects to the confirmation-required
  page (see `Spec/Bugs/BUGS-registration-confirmation-redirect.ospec`).
- The password is hashed at most once per login attempt.
- `Login.cshtml.cs` contains no manual `CheckPasswordAsync`/`IsLockedOutAsync` calls.
- `EmailConfirmationLinkSender` is the single place confirmation-email links are generated and
  sent; `Register.cshtml.cs`, `ResendEmailConfirmation.cshtml.cs`, and `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`
  all call it instead of duplicating the sequence.
- A new test covers a locked-out **and** unconfirmed account together: a wrong password returns
  the generic invalid message (not a redirect to `./Lockout`), and a correct password produces
  whichever result the framework's `PreSignInCheck` ordering resolves to first (confirmation
  before lockout).

### Out of Scope
- Any change to lockout-counting behavior (`AccessFailedAsync`, `lockoutOnFailure` threshold
  policy) — deferred to a future issue.
- `ExternalLoginSignInAsync`'s ordering (used by `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs`) — left untouched.
  It never calls `CheckPasswordSignInAsync`, since there's no password to guess in an external
  login (completing the provider's OAuth handshake already proves account ownership), so the
  oracle this refactor closes doesn't apply there.
